e-the-tools","content":"Configure the tools"},"632":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro#intro","content":"Keep in mind that the aim of this guide is not to provide a complete overview of all the commands in the k8s ecosystem but merely to give an easy to read handbook on very basic stuff"},"633":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro#tools-needed","content":"If you want to monitor or make changes to the pods:"},"634":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro#tools-needed","content":"Cloudflare WARP and an SSH client (`kubectl` runs on the node)"},"635":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro#tools-needed","content":"If you want to manage secrets:"},"636":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro#tools-needed","content":"Azure CLI"},"637":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro#tools-needed","content":"If you want to make changes to the infrastructure:"},"638":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro#tools-needed","content":"Terraform"},"639":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro#tools-needed","content":"Pre-Commits"},"640":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro#tools-needed","content":"Ansible, only for changes to the node itself"},"641":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro#configure-the-tools","content":"Connect WARP to PoliNetwork's Cloudflare Zero Trust organization"},"642":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro#configure-the-tools","content":"SSH into the node with ```ssh pnadmin@10.43.1.4```"},"643":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro#configure-the-tools","content":"Run kubectl as ```k3s kubectl``` (or add the alias ```alias kubectl='k3s kubectl'```)"},"644":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro#configure-the-tools","content":"Login in Azure CLI using az login"},"645":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/k8s-basics/intro#configure-the-tools","content":"The full procedure is in Setup."},"646":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd","type":"page","content":"CD","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Infrastructure","Getting Started","Pipelines"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd"},"647":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd-0","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd#continuous-delivery","content":"Continuous Delivery"},"648":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd-1","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd#continuous-deployment","content":"Continuous Deployment"},"649":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd-2","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd#continuous-delivery","content":"Each app repository builds its Docker image and publishes it to GHCR with the `latest` tag (see CI).\nThat's where the app pipeline stops: it doesn't touch the polinetwork-cd repository."},"650":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd-3","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd#continuous-deployment","content":"We rely on Flux to deploy our pods following the manifests in the `main` branch of polinetwork-cd.\nFlux continuously compares the cluster with the repository and applies any difference, so a merged PR in polinetwork-cd reaches production within minutes."},"651":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd-4","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd#continuous-deployment","content":"New images don't need a commit: when a new `latest` image is published, GitHub calls a Flux webhook, Flux resolves `latest` to the new image digest and rolls out the deployment.\nIf the webhook is missed, Flux checks for new images every 6 hours anyway."},"652":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd-5","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/cd#continuous-deployment","content":"Our deployments use rolling updates with `maxUnavailable: 0`: Kubernetes starts the new pod, waits for its readiness probe, and only then stops the old one, so a broken image doesn't take the app down.\nStateful services (databases, Redis, Grafana) use the `Recreate` strategy instead, because only one pod at a time can write to their volume."},"653":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci","type":"page","content":"CI","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Infrastructure","Getting Started","Pipelines"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci"},"654":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci-0","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci#continuous-integration","content":"Continuous Integration"},"655":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci-1","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci#continuous-integration","content":"The first step of our CI processes is the GitHub Actions workflow in each repository. For example, the web repository has:"},"656":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci-2","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci#continuous-integration","content":"`test.yml`, which typechecks and lints pushes to `main` and pull requests;"},"657":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci-3","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci#continuous-integration","content":"`docker.yml`, which on every push to `main` builds the Docker image for `amd64` and `arm64` and publishes it to GHCR as `ghcr.io/polinetworkorg/web:latest`."},"658":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci-4","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci#continuous-integration","content":"Our K3s node is ARM64, so the `arm64` build is required. Keep publishing a\nmulti-arch `latest` manifest when you set up a new repository."},"659":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci-5","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/pipelines/ci#continuous-integration","content":"The polinetwork-cd repository has its own CI too: the **Ansible** workflow lints the playbooks and builds every Flux path with `kustomize` on each pull request. See K3s Node."},"660":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","type":"page","content":"Setup","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Infrastructure","Getting Started"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup"},"661":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-0","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#how-access-works","content":"How access works"},"662":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-1","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#cloudflare-warp--ssh","content":"Cloudflare WARP & SSH"},"663":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-2","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#azure-cli","content":"Azure CLI"},"664":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-3","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#web-dashboards","content":"Web dashboards"},"665":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-4","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#port-forwarding","content":"Port Forwarding"},"666":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-5","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","content":"If it's your first time accessing the infrastructure, you'll need to set up the tools and configure them."},"667":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-6","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","content":"This tutorial assumes your account has the right permissions to access the infrastructure. If not, please contact the team.\nSSH access to the node is restricted to the **IT Lead** group."},"668":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-7","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#how-access-works","content":"The K3s node `k3s01` has **no public IP**. Its private address is `10.43.1.4`,\ninside the Azure VNet `vnet-k3s`, and the Azure firewall (NSG) denies all inbound\ntraffic. Nothing reaches the VM directly from the Internet:"},"669":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-8","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#how-access-works","content":"websites reach the cluster through a **Cloudflare Tunnel** (an outbound connection\nopened by `cloudflared` pods running in the cluster);"},"670":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-9","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#how-access-works","content":"people reach the VM through **Cloudflare WARP**, which routes `10.43.1.4` through\nthe same tunnel, and **Cloudflare Access for Infrastructure**, which signs a\nshort-lived SSH certificate after you log in with your PoliNetwork account."},"671":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-10","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#how-access-works","content":"There is no kubeconfig to download: you SSH into the node and run `kubectl` there."},"672":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-11","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#cloudflare-warp--ssh","content":"Install the Cloudflare WARP client."},"673":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-12","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#cloudflare-warp--ssh","content":"Enroll it in PoliNetwork's Cloudflare Zero Trust organization\n(*Preferences → Account → Login to Cloudflare Zero Trust*) and log in with your\nPoliNetwork account. Ask the team for the organization (team) name."},"674":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-13","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#cloudflare-warp--ssh","content":"With WARP connected, SSH into the node:"},"675":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-14","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#cloudflare-warp--ssh","content":"The first time, your browser asks you to authenticate. You don't need an SSH key:\nCloudflare issues a short-lived certificate for the `pnadmin` user, and the node\nonly accepts keys and Cloudflare Access certificates (no passwords, no root login)."},"676":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-15","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#cloudflare-warp--ssh","content":"To check if everything is working, run `k3s kubectl get pods -n <namespace>`. You should see a list of pods."},"677":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-16","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#cloudflare-warp--ssh","content":"`pnadmin` is in the `k3s-admin` group, which can read the K3s kubeconfig, so\n`k3s kubectl` works without `sudo`. Add an alias to save some typing:"},"678":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-17","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#cloudflare-warp--ssh","content":"The rest of the docs write `kubectl` and assume this alias."},"679":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-18","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#cloudflare-warp--ssh","content":"The possible values of ```<namespace>``` match the folders in\n`apps/` in the\npolinetwork-cd repository (e.g. `web`, `backend`, `postgres`). Run\n`kubectl get namespaces` to see them all."},"680":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-19","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#cloudflare-warp--ssh","content":"If Cloudflare is down or the tunnel is broken, you can still reach the VM through\n**Azure Run Command** or the **Serial Console** in the Azure portal (VM `k3s01`,\nresource group `rg-polinetwork`). Use them only for emergencies."},"681":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-20","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#azure-cli","content":"You still need the Azure CLI to manage secrets in Key Vault and to inspect Azure\nresources."},"682":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-21","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#azure-cli","content":"Download and install the Azure CLI."},"683":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-22","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#azure-cli","content":"Run ```az login``` to login."},"684":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-23","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#azure-cli","content":"You don't need `az aks get-credentials` or `kubelogin` anymore: those were only\nneeded for the old AKS cluster."},"685":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-24","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#web-dashboards","content":"| URL                  | What                                                            | Who can log in                      |\n| -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| flux.polinetwork.org | Flux Web UI: sync status of every app, reconcile/suspend/resume | Direttivo, via auth.polinetwork.org |","table":"table-0"},"686":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-25","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#web-dashboards","content":"| URL                        | What                                    | Who can log in   |\n| -------------------------- | --------------------------------------- | ---------------- |\n| monitoring.polinetwork.org | Grafana dashboards (Prometheus metrics) | Grafana accounts |","table":"table-0"},"687":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-26","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#web-dashboards","content":"| URL                    | What                    | Who can log in |\n| ---------------------- | ----------------------- | -------------- |\n| status.polinetwork.org | Uptime Kuma status page | Public         |","table":"table-0"},"688":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-27","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#port-forwarding","content":"To access services running on the cluster (such as the PostgreSQL database), you\nneed two hops: `kubectl port-forward` on the node, and an SSH tunnel from your\nmachine to the node. You can do both with one command:"},"689":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-28","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#port-forwarding","content":"For example, to reach PostgreSQL on `localhost:5432`:"},"690":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-29","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#port-forwarding","content":"Keep the command running while you use the connection, and stop it with `Ctrl+C`."},"691":{"id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup-30","page_id":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/setup#port-forwarding","content":"Run `kubectl get svc -n <namespace>` on the node to find service names and ports.\nYou can use the same template to connect to other services."},"692":{"id":"/docs/intro","page_id":"/docs/intro","type":"page","content":"PoliNetwork's Technical Documentation","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/intro"},"693":{"id":"/docs/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/intro#introduction","content":"Introduction"},"694":{"id":"/docs/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/intro#infrastructure","content":"Infrastructure"},"695":{"id":"/docs/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/intro#archived-projects-️","content":"Archived Projects 🗃️"},"696":{"id":"/docs/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/intro#introduction","content":"Welcome to PoliNetwork's technical documentation! This documentation is intended\nto provide a somewhat comprehensive guide to many of PoliNetwork's projects."},"697":{"id":"/docs/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/intro#infrastructure","content":"We run our infrastructure on Azure (paid with the\nMicrosoft Azure Sponsorship)"},"698":{"id":"/docs/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/intro#infrastructure","content":"If you want to learn how it works and how to add new services, check out the\nInfrastructure section."},"699":{"id":"/docs/int