oduction K3s platform. It defines:"],[564,"**`environments/legacy`:** everything from before the migration: the old AKS\ncluster and its Kubernetes/Helm resources (ArgoCD, Longhorn, Kubernetes\nDashboard, MariaDB, ...), plus shared resources such as the resource group\n`rg-polinetwork`, the backup storage account `polinetworkbackups` and the budgets."],[250,"**`ReadWriteOnce` doesn't mean one pod.** It limits a volume to one node, and we only\nhave one node: two pods can mount the same volume and write to it at the same time.\nMake sure only one pod at a time uses it (see `Recreate` below)."],[590,"**`terraform apply`**  \nApplies the changes defined in your Terraform configuration to the cloud environment.\nIn practice the Terraform workflow does this for you after the `production` approval."],[591,"**`terraform destroy`**  \nDestroys the resources managed by Terraform, useful for tearing down test environments or decommissioning infrastructure."],[587,"**`terraform init`**  \nInitializes the working directory containing the Terraform configuration files."],[589,"**`terraform plan`**  \nGenerates an execution plan showing what changes will be applied to the infrastructure."],[429,"**Additional Resources**"],[584,"**Apply the Changes:**  \nAfter your changes have been reviewed and approved, merge the pull request.\nThe push to `stable` runs the plans again, then waits for a maintainer to\napprove the GitHub **`production`** environment. Once approved, it applies\n`k3s` first and `legacy` second. You don't run `terraform apply` yourself."],[548,"**Apply:** Executing `terraform apply` implements the changes, creating or updating the infrastructure accordingly."],[334,"**Apps with a volume** use `strategy: Recreate` instead (see Add Storage)."],[446,"**Automatically update applications** when a new `latest` image is published"],[540,"**Automation:** Terraform automates the provisioning and modification of resources, streamlining our deployment processes."],[554,"**Azure State:**  \nThis is the actual state of resources deployed in the Azure environment.\nWhile Terraform keeps track of these resources via its state file, the real\nconfiguration and runtime details exist within Azure. The Terraform state acts\nas an intermediary, ensuring that the desired state (from the Terraform code)\nand the actual state (in Azure) are in sync. If resources are modified directly\nin Azure without updating Terraform, discrepancies may occur, highlighting the\nimportance of using Terraform as the single source of truth."],[539,"**Consistency:** Defining our infrastructure as code ensures that every environment is configured the same way, reducing the chance of human error."],[582,"**Cost Estimation with Infracost:**  \nWhen you open a pull request, our **Infracost workflow** is triggered\nautomatically. This workflow calculates the estimated cost impact of your\nchanges and posts a report in the pull request comments, so you can review\nany potential cost implications before merging."],[445,"**Define application deployments** in Kubernetes (plain manifests organized with Kustomize, or Helm charts)"],[585,"**Drift Detection:**  \nA nightly **Drift workflow** runs to detect any discrepancies between the\ncurrent Terraform state and the actual state in Azure. If this workflow\ndetects a drift from what the `terraform plan` output would predict, it\nautomatically opens an issue for further investigation.\nAt the moment it only checks the `legacy` root."],[579,"**Edit the Terraform Code:**  \nModify the relevant configuration files to define your desired changes in the\ninfrastructure. Most changes belong to `environments/k3s`."],[425,"**Key Difference: Infrastructure vs. Deployment**"],[428,"**Links to Repositories**"],[447,"**Manage application configurations** (environment variables, secrets pulled from Key Vault)"],[542,"**Modularity:** Terraform encourages breaking down the infrastructure into reusable modules, which simplifies management and enhances maintainability."],[448,"**Monitor the state of applications** and ensure they remain in sync with the repository"],[79,"**Naming conventions**"],[435,"**Networks and subnets** (Virtual Network, NAT Gateway for outbound traffic, Network Security Group)"],[581,"**Open a Pull Request against `stable`:**  \nThe **Terraform workflow** runs format, init, validate and plan for both roots\nand posts one comment per root with the plan. Pull requests never apply."],[547,"**Plan:** Running `terraform plan` generates an execution plan, showing you the changes Terraform will make to achieve the desired state."],[498,"**Pod and Service CIDRs** are `10.52.0.0/16` and `10.53.0.0/16`. The K3s\ndefault `10.43.0.0/16` would collide with the Azure VNet."],[424,"**PoliNetwork-CD: Definition and Purpose**"],[333,"**Probes.** The readiness probe is what makes rolling updates safe: without it,\nKubernetes kills the old pod before the new one is serving."],[80,"**Problemi comuni con il type system**"],[332,"**Requests and limits.** Everything shares one node, so every container declares\nits memory request and limit. Base the numbers on what the app actually uses\n(`kubectl top pods`)."],[580,"**Review Your Changes Locally:**  \nRun `terraform -chdir=environments/<root> plan` to generate an execution plan.\nThis command will show you what changes will be made to align the actual\ninfrastructure with your code."],[501,"**Secrets encryption** at rest is enabled."],[438,"**Security services** (Key Vaults, managed identities, workload identity federation)"],[499,"**ServiceLB** and the bundled **local-storage** are disabled. Storage comes from\nthe local-path provisioner managed by Flux."],[437,"**Storage** (Managed Disks for the node, Blob Storage for backups and Terraform state)"],[552,"**Terraform Code:**  \nThis consists of the configuration files that describe your desired\ninfrastructure. These files are the blueprint for what you want the infrastructure to look like."],[553,"**Terraform State:**  \nThe state file (we have two of them, see State Management) records the current state\nof the infrastructure as managed by Terraform. This file is critical because\nit maps your configuration to the real-world resources, tracks dependencies,\nand allows Terraform to detect what needs to change during updates. Our state\nfile is stored remotely (in an Azure Storage Account) and is locked using\nAzure's integrated leasing mechanism to prevent concurrent modifications."],[423,"**Terraform: Definition and Purpose**"],[249,"**The size is not enforced.** `storage: 4Gi` in a PVC is a label, not a quota: a volume can\ngrow until the whole disk is full, and then every app on that disk breaks.\nCheck the free space first, and keep an eye on the `PersistentVolumeHigh` and\n`NodeDiskHigh` alerts."],[550,"**THIS CAN INCUR COSTS OR CAUSE DOWNTIME AND PERMANENT DATA LOSS IF NOT DONE PROPERLY.**"],[182,"**THIS IS JUST A DOCUMENT DUMP**"],[541,"**Version Control:** Storing Terraform configurations in version control systems allows us to track changes, collaborate effectively, and revert to previous versions if needed."],[436,"**Virtual machines** (the `k3s01` K3s node)"],[251,"**Volumes are retained.** Deleting a PVC doesn't delete its directory (`reclaimPolicy: Retain`)."],[546,"**Write:** You define the infrastructure in configuration files (typically with a `.tf` extension)."],[43,"# ToDo Backend"],[16,"#1B2132\n#232A3E\n#677098\n#AFB5D9"],[18,"#454773\n#B7BCD1\n#DBDFF2\n#FFFFFF"],[58,"• Notifiche\n• Componenti riciclabili il più possibile\n• Pagine, “single-use components”\n• Internazionalizzazione\n• Da dove prendiamo i dati (mockup) - Arme"],[456,"`cloudflared` forwards it to **Traefik**, the K3s ingress controller."],[412,"`clusters/k3s/apps/<app>.yaml`: for apps with automatic updates this is a\n`ResourceSet` instead of a plain Kustomization. It generates the app's\nKustomization with an `images` override that pins the digest."],[398,"`clusters/k3s/apps/kustomization.yaml` lists the apps Flux runs. An app file\nthat is not listed there does nothing."],[382,"`clusters/k3s`"],[394,"`dependsOn`: what must be ready first. Apps wait for the secret stores, storage\nand Traefik; `backend` also waits for `postgres` and `redis`."],[657,"`docker.yml`, which on every push to `main` builds the Docker image for `amd64` and `arm64` and publishes it to GHCR as `ghcr.io/polinetworkorg/web:latest`."],[410,"`infrastructure/image-automation/image-providers.yaml`: one\n`ResourceSetInputProvider` per image, which reads the `latest` tag and its digest."],[411,"`infrastructure/image-automation/receiver.yaml`: a Flux `Receiver` called by\nthe PoliNetworkOrg organization webhook on `package` events, at\n`flux-webhook.polinetwork.org`. The webhook's HMAC signature is its only\nauthentication. If the webhook doesn't fire, the providers poll every 6 hours anyway."],[397,"`interval: 30m`: a full reconciliation at least every 30 minutes, even if Git\ndidn't change."],[256,"`name` should identify the app where the volume will be used"],[255,"`namespace` k8s namespace (set by the app's `kustomization.yaml`)"],[677,"`pnadmin` is in the `k3s-admin` group, which can read the K3s kubeconfig, so\n`k3s kubectl` works without `sudo`. Add an alias to save some typing:"],[395,"`prune: true`: a resource removed from Git is deleted from the cluster."],[298,"`restore/` now contains `postgres-dumpall.sql`, `redis.rdb`, `bot-ts-redis.rdb`,\n`influxdb-backup.tar`, `uptime-kuma.db`, `grafana.db` and the `*-files.tar` archives."],[258,"`size` expected size of the volume. Check resource units.\n  e.g. `300M`, `1Gi`, `200Mi`"],[257,"`storage-class` `fast` or `standard` (see the table above)"],[656,"`test.yml`, which typechecks and lints pushes to `main` and pull requests;"],[396,"`wait: true`: the Kustomization is ready only when all its resources are healthy."],[461,"<Card title=\"Flux\" href=\"/docs/infrastructure/getting-started/cloud-infrastructure/flux\" descripti