quanto riguarda reazioni a articoli, materiali da scaricare per orientare meglio l’utente\nanche in base a chi già ha letto/scaricato quel tipo di documento."],[70,"Suddivisione compiti:\nFrontend & logica: Elia P. – Tommaso M. – Luke P. – Giacomo M. – Federico G. (Jolly)\nAPI & Backend: Diego A. – Federico A. – Paolo R.\nDesign: tutto il resto\nPR: Giulia"],[109,"Swipe for PoliTinder - PoliMeet"],[35,"TabellaGroups"],[36,"TabelleArticle"],[37,"TabelleCalendar"],[279,"takes consistent online copies (no app is stopped);"],[3,"TASTO AGGIORNA"],[517,"Terraform"],[638,"Terraform"],[522,"Terraform Code vs. Terraform State vs. Azure State"],[467,"Terraform Documentation"],[595,"Terraform Documentation"],[523,"Terraform Implementation Overview"],[533,"Terraform is an open-source Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tool developed by\nHashiCorp. It allows you to define, provision, and manage the infrastructure\nusing a declarative configuration language. This means you describe the desired\nstate of the infrastructure, and Terraform takes care of creating and updating\nthe resources to match that state."],[545,"Terraform operates through a simple yet powerful workflow:"],[462,"Terraform Repository"],[598,"Terraform Repository"],[440,"Terraform stops at the Azure boundary: it creates the VM, but it doesn't configure\nwhat runs inside it."],[511,"The **Ansible** workflow in polinetwork-cd runs on every pull request that\ntouches `ansible/`, `clusters/k3s/`, `infrastructure/`, `apps/` or `tests/`. It\nruns `ansible-lint`, `shellcheck`, a playbook syntax check, `kustomize build` on\nevery Flux path and the manifest tests in `tests/`. It does **not** touch the VM."],[441,"The **PoliNetwork-CD** (Continuous Deployment) repository picks up where Terraform stops. It has two parts:"],[557,"The **polinetworkorg/terraform** repository is our centralized solution for\nprovisioning and managing cloud infrastructure on Azure. Its default branch is\n**`stable`**."],[434,"The **Terraform** repository is responsible for managing and provisioning cloud infrastructure. In simple terms, it defines and creates the resources needed for the infrastructure, such as:"],[328,"The `<k8s-namespace>` should be a slug (lowercase words separated by hyphens) identifier of the application/service you want to deploy."],[287,"The `BackupStale` alert fires on Telegram when there has been no successful\napplication backup for 7 hours or no control-plane backup for 26 hours.\n`BackupMetricsMissing` fires if the metrics disappear altogether."],[359,"The `config.json` files with `\"disabled\": true` you may find at the root of polinetwork-cd\nbelong to the old ArgoCD setup. Flux doesn't read them."],[415,"The `flux` CLI isn't installed on the node, but the Flux resources are regular\nKubernetes objects:"],[560,"the `k3s01` VM (Debian 13 ARM64) and its two data disks, `disk-k3s-fast`\n(Premium SSD v2, 64 GB) and `disk-k3s-standard` (Standard SSD, 128 GB);"],[259,"The `kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/prune: disabled` annotation keeps Flux from deleting the\nPVC if it disappears from Git (e.g. a bad refactor). CI rejects PVCs without it."],[330,"The `prune: disabled` annotation keeps Flux from deleting the namespace, and with it\neverything inside, if the app is removed from Git by mistake."],[260,"The `Recreate` strategy avoids two pods writing to the same volume during a rollout."],[375,"The AKS cluster `aks-polinetwork` is still defined in the `environments/legacy`\nTerraform root, but no production workload runs on it anymore.\nIf you find instructions mentioning `az aks get-credentials`, ArgoCD,\n`SecretProviderClass` or Longhorn (e.g. in old READMEs), they refer to the AKS setup."],[502,"The API server signs service-account tokens for a public OIDC issuer\n(`pnk3soidc` storage account). Azure trusts that issuer, which is how External\nSecrets reads Key Vault without any stored credential."],[377,"The best place to start is going through the Getting Started section to get a high level overview of how things are organized.  \nIf you're looking for a guide on how to achieve a specific outcome, you can look in the Guides."],[500,"The bundled **Traefik** stays as the ingress controller, but Flux turns its\nService into `ClusterIP`: only `cloudflared` reaches it."],[608,"the Cloudflare tunnel has no connections (all public websites are down);"],[439,"The code in **Terraform** describes these resources declaratively: it defines the desired state, and Terraform applies the necessary changes to reach that state. This repository is primarily used to **create and modify the cloud infrastructure** where applications will run."],[558,"The code is split into two independent **root modules** (the folders you run\n`terraform` in). Each root owns its resources and has its own state file; a root\ncan only read the other's resources through data sources, never declare them."],[302,"The control-plane backup is only needed to rebuild the node from scratch: it restores\nthe K3s datastore, including the service-account signing key that Azure workload\nidentity trusts."],[655,"The first step of our CI processes is the GitHub Actions workflow in each repository. For example, the web repository has:"],[675,"The first time, your browser asks you to authenticate. You don't need an SSH key:\nCloudflare issues a short-lived certificate for the `pnadmin` user, and the node\nonly accepts keys and Cloudflare Access certificates (no passwords, no root login)."],[515,"The Flux Operator is upgraded the same way (`flux_operator_*` and\n`flux_distribution_version`)."],[645,"The full procedure is in Setup."],[347,"The image must be **public** on GHCR: neither Flux nor the node use registry credentials.\nUsing the GHCR package `ghcr.io/polinetworkorg/<image>`:"],[346,"the image would **not update** if you **don't follow** this guide, even though there is `latest`:\nthe node already has an image called `nginx:latest` and has no reason to pull it again."],[351,"The inputs are named after the provider, with hyphens turned into underscores:\nthe provider `bot-ts-image` becomes `inputs.bot_ts_image`."],[572,"The integrated leasing mechanism provided\nby the Azure Storage Account is used for state locking, ensuring that concurrent\nTerraform operations do not conflict with each other."],[668,"The K3s node `k3s01` has **no public IP**. Its private address is `10.43.1.4`,\ninside the Azure VNet `vnet-k3s`, and the Azure firewall (NSG) denies all inbound\ntraffic. Nothing reaches the VM directly from the Internet:"],[469,"The k3s01 VM, how Ansible configures it, and how to run the playbooks."],[562,"the Key Vaults `kv-pn-apps` (application secrets) and `kv-pn-infra`\n(platform secrets);"],[295,"The last 7 days are on the node, in `/srv/standard/backups/k3s-applications/`,\nreadable only by root. Copy the one you need to your home:"],[563,"the managed identities, including the one External Secrets uses through\nworkload identity federation, and the public OIDC issuer the cluster's\nservice-account tokens are verified against."],[561,"the network: `vnet-k3s` (`10.43.0.0/16`), a NAT Gateway for outbound traffic,\nand an NSG that denies all inbound traffic;"],[323,"The node is ARM64. An image built only for `amd64` can't run there, and the pod fails\nwith `exec format error`. Build a multi-arch image (see the\nweb workflow\nfor an example), or check that the third-party image you use publishes `linux/arm64`."],[484,"The node is ARM64. Every image we deploy must have a `linux/arm64` variant,\notherwise the pod fails with `exec format error`. Our GitHub workflows publish\nmulti-arch images (`amd64` + `arm64`) for this reason."],[605,"the node is short on CPU, memory, disk space or inodes;"],[283,"The node only has the age **public** key. The private key (the \"identity\") is the\nsecret `backup-age-identity` in the `kv-pn-infra` Key Vault, and it must never be\ncopied to the node. Without it, the backups can't be read."],[409,"The pieces, all in polinetwork-cd:"],[507,"The playbooks run from your machine against `10.43.1.4` over SSH, so connect\nWARP first."],[659,"The polinetwork-cd repository has its own CI too: the **Ansible** workflow lints the playbooks and builds every Flux path with `kustomize` on each pull request. See K3s Node."],[679,"The possible values of ```<namespace>``` match the folders in\n`apps/` in the\npolinetwork-cd repository (e.g. `web`, `backend`, `postgres`). Run\n`kubectl get namespaces` to see them all."],[490,"The provisioning playbook (`ansible/playbooks/provision.yml`) applies these roles, in order:"],[678,"The rest of the docs write `kubectl` and assume this alias."],[610,"The rules live in `apps/monitoring/prometheus/alert-rules.yml` in polinetwork-cd."],[508,"The script is the only supported way to apply changes. It runs the playbook in\ncheck mode, then applies it twice and fails if the second run still changes\nsomething (the playbook must be idempotent). Finally it runs\n`playbooks/verify.yml`, which checks the disks, the K3s version, the firewall,\nthe SSH configuration, that Flux is ready, that pods can't reach Azure IMDS, and\nit takes and validates a fresh backup. Logs are saved in `ansible/artifacts/`\n(ignored by Git)."],[286,"The storage account only accepts traffic from the K3s subnet, so download backups from the node."],[503,"The VM has no public IP and the NSG denies all inbound traffic. The host\nfirewall drops everything except SSH, the node itself and cluster traffic."],[458,"The VM never accepts inbound connections from the Internet: the tunnel is an\noutbound connection, and outbound traffic leaves Azure through a NAT Gateway."],[261,"The volume is created when the first pod using it is scheduled, so the PVC stays\n`Pending` until then: that's expected."],[516,"The workload identity trust relies on the cluster's service-account signing key,\nwhose public part is committed in Terraform\n(`environments/k3s/k3s-service-account-jwks.json`). If the node is rebuilt from\nscratch without restoring the control-plane backup, the key changes and that file\nmust be updated, otherwise External Secrets can't read Key Vault."],[338,"Then add it to the list:"],[293,"Then run the playbook (see K3s Node):\n`verify.yml` takes a fresh backup and checks it."],[206,"There are three required steps:"],[246,"There are two storage classes, one per data disk:"],[671,"There is no kubeconfig to download: you SSH into the node and run `kubectl` there."],[216,"There is no need to set the namespace in the `ExternalSecret`: Kustomize sets it\nfrom the `namespace` field of `kustomization.yaml`."],[353,"These are some useful operations on existing apps."],[299,"These files hold personal data and credentials in clear text. Delete them as soon as\nyou're done."],[555,"This clear separation between code, Terraform state, and the actual cloud state\nis key to maintaining a reliable and predictable infrastructure."],[534,"This documentation assumes you already have the Terraform CLI installed on your\nlocal machine. If you need help setting up Terraform, please refer to the\nofficial documentation."],[588,"This has to be run as soon as you clone the repository or when you pull changes\nthat include new modules or providers. Locally, add\n`-backend-config=use_oidc=false` (OIDC is only for GitHub Actions)."],[575,"This is one of the core reasons we are structured as a nonprofit organization, and why\nwe use Azure as our cloud provider."],[324,"This is the crucial part, so make sure to follow it step-by-step."],[430,"This section explains how we organized our infrastructure."],[578,"This section provides a basic guide on how to update our infrastructure using\nTerraform and introduces some of the automated workflows that help us maintain\nconsistency and control."],[667,"This tutorial assumes your account has the right permissions to access the infrastructure. If not, please contact the team.\nSSH access to the node is restricted to the **IT Lead** group."],[615,"This w