g the manifests in the `main` branch of polinetwork-cd.\nFlux continuously compares the cluster with the repository and applies any difference, so a merged PR in polinetwork-cd reaches production within minutes."],[697,"We run our infrastructure on Azure (paid with the\nMicrosoft Azure Sponsorship)"],[569,"We use an **Azure Storage Account** for managing the Terraform state. The state\nfiles are stored within a dedicated container named `terraform-state`,\nas defined in our **storage** module:"],[313,"We use k8s to deploy our applications, on our K3s node.  \nEach app is a folder of plain k8s manifests in the\npolinetwork-cd repository, tied\ntogether with Kustomize. Helm Charts\nare possible too, through a Flux `HelmRelease` (see `infrastructure/external-secrets` for an example)."],[664,"Web dashboards"],[386,"Web UI"],[669,"websites reach the cluster through a **Cloudflare Tunnel** (an outbound connection\nopened by `cloudflared` pods running in the cluster);"],[145,"Welcome (con gruppi per semestre e anno)"],[696,"Welcome to PoliNetwork's technical documentation! This documentation is intended\nto provide a somewhat comprehensive guide to many of PoliNetwork's projects."],[556,"Welcome to the documentation for the **polinetworkorg/terraform** repository.\nThis page provides an in-depth look at our Terraform implementation on\n**Microsoft Azure**, detailing the architecture of our infrastructure and the\nkey modules that make it up. While this overview highlights our main components,\nit’s important to note that there are alternative approaches to similar problems,\nand our setup is tailored to our current needs."],[363,"What"],[472,"What Ansible sets up"],[380,"What is Flux?"],[519,"What is Terraform?"],[565,"What runs *inside* the VM is not managed here: see K3s Node and Flux."],[205,"Whenever there is `<something>` in the code or in a command, that's a parameter you need to set."],[362,"Where"],[449,"While Terraform is responsible for infrastructure, **PoliNetwork-CD** focuses on **managing the lifecycle of applications** running on that infrastructure, ensuring that code changes are applied automatically and securely."],[566,"While the choices made in this implementation reflect our specific requirements\nand constraints, it's worth noting that other approaches exist for managing\ncloud infrastructure with Terraform."],[364,"Why"],[374,"Why K3s instead of a managed cluster?\nUntil October 2026 we ran on AKS (Azure Kubernetes Service). K3s gives us the\nsame Kubernetes API on one VM that we fully control and size ourselves, and it\ndrops our dependency on AKS-specific add-ons (Azure CSI drivers, Longhorn, ArgoCD)."],[373,"Why Kubernetes on a single VM instead of plain Docker?\nKubernetes gives us declarative deployments, rolling updates, health checks and\nper-application resource limits. Flux keeps the cluster in sync with Git, so the\n`polinetwork-cd` repository is always the source of truth for what is running."],[520,"Why We Use Terraform"],[674,"With WARP connected, SSH into the node:"],[228,"You can add (or update) a secret with the following command:"],[576,"You can find more about our benefits here."],[314,"You don't deploy from your machine: you open a PR, and once it's merged into `main`,\nFlux applies it.  \nCheck minikube or K3s to run a k8s cluster on your machine for testing purposes."],[684,"You don't need `az aks get-credentials` or `kubelogin` anymore: those were only\nneeded for the old AKS cluster."],[227,"You need the Azure CLI, logged in with an account that has the\n**Key Vault Secrets Officer** role on the vault."],[506,"You rarely need this: day-to-day changes go through Flux. Run Ansible when you\nchange something under `ansible/` (e.g. a K3s upgrade or a new backup target).\nOnly the IT Lead group has access to the node."],[681,"You still need the Azure CLI to manage secrets in Key Vault and to inspect Azure\nresources."],[604,"You usually don't have to go looking for problems: Prometheus evaluates alert\nrules and Alertmanager sends them to our **Telegram** alert chat. Critical alerts\nrepeat every hour, warnings every 12 hours. Among others, you get alerted when:"],[535,"You'll also need to be logged in with the Azure CLI and have the necessary\npermissions to manage the resources in our Azure subscription (more on this\nhere)."]],"type":"string"},"page_id":{"docs":{"1":13,"2":14,"3":15,"4":16,"5":17,"6":18,"7":19,"8":20,"9":21,"10":22,"11":23,"12":24,"13":25,"14":26,"15":27,"16":28,"17":29,"18":30,"19":31,"20":32,"21":33,"22":34,"23":35,"24":36,"25":37,"26":38,"27":39,"28":40,"29":41,"30":42,"31":43,"32":44,"33":45,"34":46,"35":48,"36":49,"37":50,"38":47,"39":51,"40":52,"41":53,"42":54,"43":55,"44":56,"45":57,"46":58,"47":59,"48":60,"49":61,"50":62,"51":63,"52":64,"53":65,"54":66,"55":67,"56":68,"57":69,"58":70,"59":71,"60":72,"61":73,"62":74,"63":75,"64":76,"65":77,"66":78,"67":79,"68":80,"69":81,"70":82,"71":83,"72":84,"73":85,"74":86,"75":87,"76":88,"77":89,"78":90,"79":91,"80":92,"81":93,"82":94,"83":95,"84":96,"85":97,"86":98,"87":99,"88":100,"89":101,"90":102,"91":103,"92":104,"93":105,"94":106,"95":107,"96":108,"97":109,"98":110,"99":111,"100":112,"101":113,"102":114,"103":115,"104":116,"105":117,"106":118,"107":119,"108":120,"109":121,"110":122,"111":123,"112":124,"113":125,"114":126,"115":127,"116":128,"117":129,"118":130,"119":131,"120":132,"121":133,"122":134,"123":135,"124":136,"125":137,"126":138,"127":139,"128":140,"129":141,"130":142,"131":143,"132":144,"133":145,"134":146,"135":147,"136":148,"137":149,"138":150,"139":151,"140":152,"141":153,"142":154,"143":155,"144":156,"145":157,"146":158,"147":159,"148":160,"149":161,"150":162,"151":163,"152":164,"153":165,"154":166,"155":167,"156":168,"157":169,"158":170,"159":171,"160":172,"161":173,"162":174,"163":175,"164":176,"165":177,"166":178,"167":179,"168":180,"169":181,"170":182,"171":7,"172":8,"173":9,"174":10,"175":11,"176":12,"177":0,"178":1,"179":2,"180":3,"181":4,"182":5,"183":6,"184":49